    "text": "This is a greedy heuristic, not a provably optimal solver. It repeatedly signs up the library with the best estimated return, using a lazy priority queue.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- Each library's books are sorted by score (descending) once.\n- A library's value at time `t` is the sum of its top `min(M\u00b7rem, N)` unscanned books divided by its signup time `T`, where `rem = D \u2212 t \u2212 T` is the number of shipping days left.\n- Values only decrease as time passes and books get scanned. So stored values are upper bounds, and a lazy priority queue works: pop the best candidate, recompute its value, and accept it only if it still beats the next candidate; otherwise reinsert it.\n- When a library is selected, its books are listed in score order, marked as scanned, and `t` advances by `T`. Only unscanned books are listed, so no book is shipped twice and no shipping slot is wasted.\n\nI have not compiled or run this against the judge's data sets.\n\n```cpp\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint B, L, D;\nvector<long long> S;\nvector<int> Nj, Tj, Mj;\nvector<vector<int>> sortedBooks;\nvector<char> scanned;\n\n// Returns estimated value of library j if signed up starting at day t.\n// If out != nullptr, appends the selected unscanned books (in order) to it.\n// Returns -1 if no shipping days remain, 0 if no unscanned books.\ndouble evalLib(int j, long long t, vector<int>* out) {\n    long long rem = (long long)D - t - Tj[j];\n    if (rem <= 0) return -1.0;\n    long long k = min((long long)Mj[j] * rem, (long long)sortedBooks[j].size());\n    long long sum = 0, cnt = 0;\n    for (int b : sortedBooks[j]) {\n        if (cnt >= k) break;\n        if (scanned[b]) continue;\n        sum += S[b];\n        cnt++;\n        if (out) out->push_back(b);\n    }\n    if (cnt == 0) return 0.0;\n    return (double)sum / (double)Tj[j];\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    cin >> B >> L >> D;\n    S.assign(B, 0);\n    for (int i = 0; i < B; i++) cin >> S[i];\n\n    Nj.assign(L, 0);\n    Tj.assign(L, 0);\n    Mj.assign(L, 0);\n    sortedBooks.assign(L, {});\n    for (int j = 0; j < L; j++) {\n        cin >> Nj[j] >> Tj[j] >> Mj[j];\n        sortedBooks[j].resize(Nj[j]);\n        for (int i = 0; i < Nj[j]; i++) cin >> sortedBooks[j][i];\n        sort(sortedBooks[j].begin(), sortedBooks[j].end(), [&](int a, int b) {\n            if (S[a] != S[b]) return S[a] > S[b];\n            return a < b;\n        });\n    }\n\n    scanned.assign(B, 0);\n\n    priority_queue<pair<double, int>> pq;\n    for (int j = 0; j < L; j++) {\n        double v = evalLib(j, 0, nullptr);\n        if (v > 0) pq.push({v, j});\n    }\n\n    long long t = 0;\n    vector<pair<int, vector<int>>> result;\n\n    while (!pq.empty() && t < D) {\n        auto top = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n        int j = top.second;\n        double nv = evalLib(j, t, nullptr);\n        if (nv <= 0) continue;\n        if (!pq.empty() && nv < pq.top().first) {\n            pq.push({nv, j});\n            continue;\n        }\n        // Select library j.\n        vector<int> books;\n        evalLib(j, t, &books);\n        for (int b : books) scanned[b] = 1;\n        result.push_back({j, books});\n        t += Tj[j];\n    }\n\n    // Output.\n    string out;\n    out.reserve(1 << 20);\n    out += to_string(result.size());\n    out += '\\n';\n    for (auto& r : result) {\n        out += to_string(r.first);\n        out += ' ';\n        out += to_string(r.second.size());\n        out += '\\n';\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < r.second.size(); i++) {\n            if (i) out += ' ';\n            out += to_string(r.second[i]);\n        }\n        out += '\\n';\n    }\n    fwrite(out.data(), 1, out.size(), stdout);\n    return 0;\n}\n```",
